  "title": "Mephisto Modular Autosensory Chess Board",
  "year": "1983",
  "subtitle": "A durable pressure-sensing chess board that outlives generations of plug-in processors — the physical interface as a long-term investment",
  "why_scout_cares": "The Mephisto Modular system (Hegener & Glaser, introduced 1983) is the museum's cleanest example of hardware modularity as an HCI design choice: a single autosensory chess board whose pressure-sensitive squares are the input device, onto which the owner snaps different processor/program/display modules over years. The board is a durable physical platform — the interface — while the ever-improving computation travels in swappable cartridges. It inverts the usual 'computer as disposable whole' framing of the era. The interaction itself is embodied: you input a move by simply pressing a chess piece down on a square (autosensory pressure switches), no coordinate keypad. This is distinct from the museum's Fidelity Voice Sensory Chess Challenger (sensory board + speech) and Novag Robot Adversary (robotic arm) because the point is the board-outlives-modules modular philosophy, not the sensing or the robot. Richard Lang's Mephisto programs won six World Computer Chess Championships (1984-1990), and by 1989 over 90% of chess computers sold in Germany were Mephistos.",
